    <string name="chapter_portage_intro">1836. Boats can't run the rapids between the lower lake and the upper one, so cargo is unloaded at Port Ellery, carted over the portage road and loaded again at Carrick Landing. Lay a short line through the gorge beside the rapids and run a train on it.\n\nCarry people between the two landings, and bring timber from the camp on the east shore to the sawmill at Port Ellery. You have until the end of 1846.</string>
    <string name="chapter_trunk_intro">1856. Five towns are on the north shore of the lake: Ashcombe in the west, Saint-Évrard in the east, and Marlowe, the biggest, between them. In winter the lake freezes. Lay a line along the shore. Four rivers come down to the lake, and each needs a bridge.\n\nCarry passengers from both ends to Marlowe, and help Marlowe grow to 700 people, by the end of 1868. A stage company already runs coaches along the shore road.</string>
    <string name="chapter_last_spike_intro">1881. Between Port Haldane on the west coast and Fort Calder on the prairie are two ranges of mountains, a long valley, and only two passes low enough to climb. The Western Slope Railway is building east from the coast, so join your line to it. The passes get deep snow, and avalanches come down the steep slopes.\n\nCarry 1,000 passengers a year between Port Haldane and Fort Calder, and bring 8,000 tonnes of coal from the mine in the valley to the port, by the end of 1886.</string>
    <string name="chapter_seaway_intro">1954. A lake in the west drains by a long river to the sea in the east, but halfway the river falls over a ledge of rock. Ships from the lake go no further, and ships from the sea turn back below the rapids. Dig a canal across the ledge, with two locks up onto it at each end, so ships can run the whole river. Ice closes it every winter.\n\nBring 15,000 tonnes of iron ore from the mine north of the lake to the steel mill downstream, and 12,000 tonnes of grain from the farms by the lake to the port at the river mouth, by the end of 1964.</string>
    <string name="chapter_iron_ore_intro">1955. Iron ore lies under Carrow Lake, inland on the Shield. From there to the sea at Baie-Clairval it's rock, muskeg and lakes, with no road and no rail. Build a railway from the mine to the coast, over the rock and round the lakes. The snow comes early.\n\nBring 15,000 tonnes of ore to the steel mill at Baie-Clairval, and help the town grow to 650 people, by the end of 1965.</string>
    <string name="chapter_high_speed_intro">2032. Westbridge and Montrevel, the two biggest cities in the province, are at either end of a long lake shore, with smaller towns along the way. Corridor Coachways runs coaches on the highway between them. Build a fast line along the shore. The fastest trains do 200 km/h, on straight, level track.\n\nCarry 11,000 passengers a year between Westbridge and Montrevel, and raise the company's worth $100,000 from its lowest, by the end of 2042.</string>
    <string name="chapter_warming_north_intro">2062. Winters in the north are shorter every year. Tamarack Point and Fort Delorme are on lakes in the tundra, reached in winter by ice roads over the lakes, and those close earlier each spring. The railhead at Fort Ashdown is far to the south. The towns only grow when they get people moving about, mail, food all year, and fuel for heat through the winter once they're big enough.\n\nKeep both northern towns supplied and help each grow to 550 people by the end of 2072.</string>
